And I'll present just a couple of things and then we can talk through some details if you'd like.

00:40:55.835 --> 00:40:56.555
Absolutely.

00:40:56.875 --> 00:40:57.755
Okay, so

00:40:58.715 --> 00:41:00.475
affordable housing is

00:41:00.635 --> 00:41:01.595
defined

00:41:01.755 --> 00:41:02.635
formally

00:41:02.875 --> 00:41:04.795
by the state

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:06.360
as

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:08.360
housing that is

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:11.000
available to

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:12.200
residents

00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:14.760
spending less than

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:16.360
30%

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:19.000
of their annual household income on housing.

00:41:19.555 --> 00:41:28.435
If you're spending more than 30% of your annual household income on housing, whether it's an ownership situation with a mortgage or a rental situation in your apartment,

00:41:28.755 --> 00:41:29.875
you're considered

00:41:29.955 --> 00:41:30.915
cost burden.

00:41:31.555 --> 00:41:32.115
And

00:41:33.155 --> 00:41:33.955
the state

00:41:34.450 --> 00:41:35.650
has thresholds

00:41:35.650 --> 00:41:37.490
of 80%, 60%

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:42.050
of the area median income that it labels as moderately affordable,

00:41:42.450 --> 00:41:46.770
or I'm sorry, moderate income, low income, and then there's, you know, very low income and that sort of thing.

00:41:47.635 --> 00:41:54.595
So the state has sort of definitional thresholds for what it means when it refers to affordable housing, but it's generally

00:41:55.235 --> 00:41:59.235
pegged to a percentage of the median income and 30%

00:41:59.235 --> 00:42:00.835
of that income spent on housing.

00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:04.920
There are actually very few

00:42:05.160 --> 00:42:08.040
specific requirements that any municipality

00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:11.720
has relative to fair or affordable housing.

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:15.320
One of those is the Fair Housing Act, which was adopted

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:19.295
around fifty five years ago, fifty three years ago.

00:42:19.775 --> 00:42:21.695
That basically just mandates

00:42:21.695 --> 00:42:26.415
in sort of broad terms that every unit of government, state, county, town

00:42:28.095 --> 00:42:29.295
should affirmatively

00:42:29.890 --> 00:42:31.010
act to

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:33.330
further fair housing goals.

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:40.610
What that means, what does it mean if a town is is doing this in good faith? There is not has not really been

00:42:41.090 --> 00:42:41.970
legislated

00:42:41.970 --> 00:42:48.645
that much, particularly in Connecticut. But there is sort of a fair housing mandate out there in the federal government for five decades.

00:42:49.285 --> 00:42:50.405
More locally,

00:42:51.125 --> 00:42:52.965
the Connecticut general statutes

00:42:53.045 --> 00:42:55.125
require a couple of things

00:42:55.285 --> 00:42:56.805
of municipalities,

00:42:57.045 --> 00:42:58.085
largely

00:42:58.085 --> 00:43:00.930
acting through the planning and zoning commission.

00:43:01.650 --> 00:43:02.930
The first is

00:43:03.250 --> 00:43:11.010
mandating that a town does a plan of conservation and development every ten years. And one of my first tasks as joining

00:43:11.010 --> 00:43:19.335
the town as a consulting planner is to help update this process. The last plan of conservation and development in Andover was adopted in 2015.

00:43:19.575 --> 00:43:23.655
So we're we're due to adopt one in 2025 and that's our target.

00:43:24.215 --> 00:43:30.580
And one of the requirements of that plan of conservation and development is to plan for

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:33.540
specifically multifamily and affordable housing,

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:38.340
not only in the town, but sort of looking at affordable housing regionally.

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:47.925
So exactly what form that takes or how that consideration is given to the mandatory plan of conservation and development is left a little vague, but the POCD

00:43:47.925 --> 00:43:49.045
must consider

00:43:49.125 --> 00:43:50.005
these things.

00:43:51.445 --> 00:43:56.700
In addition, zoning regulations, when the town writes or updates its zoning regulations,

00:43:57.420 --> 00:44:02.140
the zoning enabling statute, which is section eight dash two of the Connecticut General Statutes,

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:02.860
requires

00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:04.940
that a municipality

00:44:04.785 --> 00:44:09.425
have due consideration for multifamily housing and affordable housing.

00:44:09.905 --> 00:44:12.705
And again, what that means, what that looks like,

00:44:14.065 --> 00:44:19.585
you know, what's the difference between an Andover and a Manchester or a Little Mantic and how does each town,

00:44:20.890 --> 00:44:24.250
each town's responsibility for including this in their zoning,

00:44:24.970 --> 00:44:26.170
it's vaguely defined.

00:44:26.330 --> 00:44:28.170
And so it's sort of town by town

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:33.850
up to the town to figure out, up to each planning zoning commission to figure out how its regulations will

00:44:34.185 --> 00:44:39.705
consider and reflect consideration for for multifamily and and affordable housing.

00:44:40.985 --> 00:44:42.105
And the last

00:44:42.505 --> 00:44:43.625
must do

00:44:43.785 --> 00:44:48.025
relative to affordable housing is a relatively new law passed in 2017,

00:44:48.650 --> 00:44:51.450
which is section eight dash 30 J

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:57.850
of the Connecticut General Statutes that requires every municipality in the state every five years to

00:44:58.090 --> 00:45:04.295
produce or update an affordable housing plan, which is also sort of vaguely

00:45:04.535 --> 00:45:05.335
defined,

00:45:05.335 --> 00:45:13.095
but it basically says every municipality has to come up with a five year plan to increase the number of affordable housing developments in that municipality.

00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:18.210
It doesn't say by how many, it doesn't say by what percentage, it doesn't say what type

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:19.250
apartments,

00:45:19.410 --> 00:45:20.290
condos,

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:21.730
accessory apartments,

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:28.370
senior housing, it doesn't say any of those things. It just says come up with a way to produce more.

00:45:30.945 --> 00:45:32.225
And the town of

00:45:32.785 --> 00:45:33.665
Andover

00:45:33.665 --> 00:45:34.785
completed this,

00:45:35.665 --> 00:45:36.385
I believe,

00:45:36.705 --> 00:45:38.625
oh, Jed, about a year and a half ago.

00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:44.800
And so the town has its first affordable housing plan

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:48.320
that was adopted and has a number of recommendations

00:45:48.320 --> 00:45:49.920
for what can be done

00:45:50.320 --> 00:45:51.040
to

00:45:52.240 --> 00:45:53.920
create an atmosphere of

00:45:54.705 --> 00:45:59.185
having more affordable housing in town. So those are the must dos.

00:46:00.225 --> 00:46:01.345
Then there are

00:46:01.985 --> 00:46:03.105
sort of the

00:46:04.545 --> 00:46:05.425
or else,

00:46:05.745 --> 00:46:07.880
the stick, in which in many

00:46:08.200 --> 00:46:08.920
cases

00:46:09.080 --> 00:46:15.480
is the perception of what's called the affordable housing appeals act, which is section eight dash 30 gs

00:46:15.560 --> 00:46:16.200
of

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:17.560
the statutes,

00:46:18.280 --> 00:46:23.565
which is a law that's been in effect for some thirty five years or so. I think it was 1989,

00:46:23.565 --> 00:46:24.445
1990

00:46:24.765 --> 00:46:27.725
that it was put in place that says basically,

00:46:28.845 --> 00:46:31.085
if a municipality does not have

00:46:32.045 --> 00:46:33.805
a minimum of 10%

00:46:33.885 --> 00:46:36.610
of its housing stock designated

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:38.530
as affordable,

00:46:39.010 --> 00:46:40.450
which is defined as

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:42.610
deed restricted,

00:46:44.290 --> 00:46:45.250
income limited,

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:48.450
or government assisted housing.

00:46:49.265 --> 00:46:52.945
If it doesn't have 10% of its stock in one of those categories,

00:46:53.345 --> 00:46:54.625
it is subject

00:46:55.025 --> 00:46:55.665
to

00:46:56.065 --> 00:46:59.265
the appeals act, which would allow a developer,

00:47:00.465 --> 00:47:04.570
should they wish to put a development in town with at least 30%

00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:07.130
of the new units deeded as affordable

00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:08.570
to

00:47:08.570 --> 00:47:09.450
essentially,

00:47:09.450 --> 00:47:11.450
to sort of make a complicated

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:17.290
matter simple, to essentially do an end around around your zoning regulations to allow this development to happen.

00:47:19.675 --> 00:47:26.315
You know, and so the town generally doesn't allow, the town of Andover currently and many rural towns don't sort of allow multifamily

00:47:26.315 --> 00:47:31.835
developments as of right, and certainly don't allow higher density multifamily developments

00:47:32.155 --> 00:47:38.070
at 16 units to the acre, at 20 units to the acre or something like that. But if a developer came in

00:47:39.350 --> 00:47:43.510
and Andover at the time of application did not have 10% of its housing

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:45.190
restricted as affordable,

00:47:46.005 --> 00:47:55.845
As long as the developer could prove that such a development could be built without violating public health code or traffic concerns and things like that, it basically would be approved.

